 To achieve this, you can use a combination of several functions provided in your API:

1. `get_current_day` to get the current day and time
2. `search_calendar_events` to find the 'Introductory meeting' event on 2024-05-15
3. `get_day_calendar_events` to check if there is an event at 10:00 on 2024-05-19
4. `create_calendar_event` to create the new 'Follow-up meeting' event

Here's a sample code snippet that demonstrates how you can achieve this:

```json
{
  "name": "schedule_follow_up_meeting",
  "arguments": {
    "title": "Follow-up meeting",
    "description": "Follow-up meeting to discuss the project.",
    "start_time": "",
    "end_time": "2024-05-19T11:00",
    "location": null,
    "participants": [],
    "query": "Introductory meeting"
  },
  "code": {
    "action": "sequence",
    "actions": [
      {
        "name": "get_current_day",
        "arguments": {}
      },
      {
        "name": "search_calendar_events",
        "arguments": {
          "query": "Introductory meeting",
          "sender": null
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "get_day_calendar_events",
        "arguments": {
          "day": "$[1].start_time.split(' ')[0]" // assuming the date is in YYYY-MM-DD format
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "search_calendar_events",
        "arguments": {
          "query": "$[2][0].title",
          "sender": null,
          "date": "2024-05-19"
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "get_day_calendar_events",
        "arguments": {
          "day": "$[3][0].start_time.split(' ')[0]"
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "if",
        "arguments": {
          "condition": {
            "name": "len",
            "arguments": {
              "value": "$[4][0].start_time"
            }
          },
          "then": [
            {
              "name": "set",
              "arguments": {
                "variable": "start_time",
                "value": "$[5][0].end_time"
              }
            },
            {
              "name": "create_calendar_event",
              "arguments": {
                "title": "$[0]",
                "description": "$[1]",
                "start_time": "$[6]",
                "end_time": "$[2].end_time",
                "location": "$[5][0].location",
                "participants": "$[5][0].participants"
              }
            }
          ],
          "else": [
            {
              "name": "set",
              "arguments": {
                "variable": "start_time",
                "value": "$[2][0].end_time"
              }
            },
            {
              "name": "create_calendar_event",
              "arguments": {
                "title": "$[0]",
                "description": "$[1]",
                "start_time": "$[6]",
                "end_time": "$[7].start_time",
                "location": "$[2][0].location",
                "participants": "$[2][0].participants"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

This code snippet defines a new function `schedule_follow_up_meeting` that creates the 'Follow-up meeting' event on either 10:00 or 16:00 on 2024-05-19, depending on whether there is an event at 10:00. The function first searches for the 'Introductory meeting' event and checks if there is an event at 10:00 on 2024-05-19. If there is, it sets the start time of the new event to be the end time of the existing event. Otherwise, it sets the start time to be the end time of the 'Introductory meeting' event and creates the new event at 16:00. The participants for both events are the same as those in the 'Introductory meeting'.
